{
  "dom_id": "step-q105307",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14108272/assets/84755249e491fd44868098e8edccfd32cffda23a5859f0a9df293b57b4353040.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{-x}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $\u2200\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14108272/assets/016b83dcbb2f2008d428fb52ba26336b35fa0af0d67e435d06122845f3492b2e.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${2}^{-x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$ So, there is no value $x$ such that $f(x)=0\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
